This is, essentially, asking the Framework whether the calling package
is able to grant the permission for the URI to itself. It aligns with
how the method is used by System UI in other places. This is also a more
stable way to perform the check.

Bug: b/435188844
Test: `sts-tradefed run sts-dynamic-develop -m CtsSecurityBulletinHostTestCases -t android.security.cts.Bug_435188844`
Flag: EXEMPT CVE_FIX
Change-Id: I287cb5a32e24d4d6cbecf65271ae5159695ab477

(cherry picked from commit e3670d5711c7e806cfbdb44c6e9963a95300faba)
Change-Id: I1960fbac03804662ff462def6e5464234b9f588e
---
 .../intentresolver/domain/UriCallerReadAccessValidator.kt      | 3 ++-
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/java/src/com/android/intentresolver/domain/UriCallerReadAccessValidator.kt b/java/src/com/android/intentresolver/domain/UriCallerReadAccessValidator.kt
index 28a50f6..9a4a6bb 100644
--- a/java/src/com/android/intentresolver/domain/UriCallerReadAccessValidator.kt
+++ b/java/src/com/android/intentresolver/domain/UriCallerReadAccessValidator.kt
@@ -71,7 +71,8 @@ constructor(
 object CallerUriReadAccessValidatorModule {
     @Provides
     @PackageName
-    fun packageName(@ApplicationContext context: Context): String = context.packageName
+    fun packageName(activityModelRepository: ActivityModelRepository): String =
+        activityModelRepository.value.launchedFromPackage
 
     @Provides
     @LaunchedFromUid
